<h1>Building a Skool education business with your software development expe=
rtise</h1><p>Fundamentals, five community concepts, launch and growth playb=
ooks, and the economics of a $200K monthly target.</p><div class=3D"print-r=
ow"><button id=3D"print-report" type=3D"button">Print or save as PDF</butto=
n><a href=3D"file:///C:/Users/amallick.ENGINEERS/OneDrive%20-%20Unison%20So=
ftware,%20Inc/Documents/ChatGPT/My%20career%20growth%20and%20side%20hustle%=
20income/skool-business-report.md">Editable report source</a></div></header=
><p>Prepared for Aadil Mallick on November 13 2026. All money is USD.</p><p=
>Start with one paid project community for JavaScript and TypeScript develo=
pers. Use a browser extension sprint as the first concrete outcome, then ex=
pand into reliable AI applications after the first cohort succeeds. Treat $=
200,000 per month as a business design target for gross recurring membershi=
p revenue. It requires repeatable acquisition, measurable learning outcomes=
, ongoing reasons to renew, and a delivery team.</p><p>No plan can make cus=
tomer demand or income foolproof. You can make execution more reliable by l=
imiting early spending, collecting paid evidence, checking quality, and cha=
nging course at explicit decision points. The plan below uses those control=
s.</p><p>How to use this report: read sections 1 through 5 to understand th=
e business, compare the five community concepts in sections 6 through 10, a=
nd execute the validation and launch plan in sections 11 through 17. Use se=
ctions 18 through 23 to manage retention, economics, and scale. Templates a=
nd decision worksheets follow.</p><p>Evidence key: linked platform and publ=
ic profile facts were checked November 13 2026. Prices proposed for your of=
fers, conversion rates, budgets, scoring, and milestones are planning assum=
ptions or experiments. They are not market averages, forecasts, or verified=
 earnings. No community, advertisement, billing account, or public campaign=
 has been launched for you.</p><section id=3D"section-1"><h2>1 Business fun=
damentals you can reuse in any niche</h2><h3>Customers buy progress in a pa=
rticular situation</h3><p>A topic is a category of knowledge. An offer is a=
 useful change for a particular buyer. =E2=80=9CAI education=E2=80=9D is a =
topic. =E2=80=9CShip a document question-answering feature with a small eva=
luation set and a cost limit=E2=80=9D is a project outcome. =E2=80=9CEarn m=
ore as a developer=E2=80=9D is a desire; it needs a credible path and evide=
nce before it becomes a useful offer.</p><p>Define five things before you c=
hoose a community name:</p><ul><li>The buyer and their starting skills.</li=
><li>The situation that makes the problem urgent.</li><li>The result they c=
an inspect.</li><li>The obstacles your teaching and community remove.</li><=
li>The time, money, and effort the result requires.</li></ul><p>For an expe=
rienced JavaScript learner, the obstacle may be extension message passing a=
nd release preparation. For a freelancer, it may be scoping client work. Fo=
r a beginner, it may be understanding asynchronous code. These buyers need =
different lessons and different support.</p><h3>Demand means behavior with =
a cost</h3><p>People can sincerely like your idea without buying it. Strong=
er evidence includes spending money on a competing solution, repeatedly att=
empting the project, attending a relevant workshop, and purchasing your cle=
arly described pilot. Record the difference between compliments, email sign=
ups, attended sessions, payments, completed projects, and renewals.</p><p>A=
sk about the last time the problem occurred. A learner who says =E2=80=9CI =
should learn AI someday=E2=80=9D is different from one who tried to build a=
 tool last weekend, got stuck, and is looking for help now. Prioritize the =
second situation.</p><h3>Positioning creates a reason to choose you</h3><p>=
A useful positioning sentence is: =E2=80=9CFor [specific people] who need [=
specific progress], this community provides [method and support], backed by=
 [relevant proof].=E2=80=9D</p><p>Your proof can be a functioning extension=
, a clear technical explanation, or a learner artifact. A long skill list i=
s less persuasive because the buyer must work out how it helps them.</p><p>=
Differentiation should be a meaningful choice. You might focus on small too=
ls that work reliably, transparent engineering tradeoffs, and learners expl=
aining their own decisions. A competitor can also say =E2=80=9Cgreat commun=
ity=E2=80=9D; fewer can show your particular teaching process and reviewed =
artifacts.</p><h3>A paid community has several jobs</h3><p>The curriculum e=
xplains what to do. Exercises build ability. Feedback identifies mistakes. =
Accountability helps learners return. Peers supply examples and useful rela=
tionships. Facilitation makes those interactions happen.</p><p>A library of=
 videos can be a useful product, but recurring membership needs continuing =
value. After a member finishes the first project, what useful work happens =
next month? If you cannot answer that, sell a finite course or cohort and l=
et members leave successfully.</p><h3>Value and willingness to pay are rela=
ted but different</h3><p>A skill might be valuable over a career while a le=
arner has little current budget. A business owner might have budget but no =
time to study. The first buyer needs accessible teaching; the second may ne=
ed an implementation service. Do not force both into one subscription.</p><=
p>You can estimate value by asking about time lost, current alternatives, m=
issed deadlines, and the importance of finishing. Test willingness to pay b=
y making a real offer. Avoid pricing from imagined salary increases or hypo=
thetical client income.</p><h3>Distribution is part of the product business=
</h3><p>A valuable community cannot grow if suitable people never encounter=
 it. A channel works when it repeatedly reaches the right people at a toler=
able cost. Your content, workshops, referrals, and partnerships are mechani=
sms for creating that contact and trust.</p><p>Marketing should make a usef=
ul promise, show evidence, invite a next step, and help people judge fit. I=
t should also disqualify unsuitable buyers. Selling to someone without the =
prerequisites can raise refunds and consume delivery time.</p><h3>Retention=
 is a consequence to investigate</h3><p>People renew when the next period i=
s worth its price. They leave because of poor fit, insufficient time, succe=
ss and graduation, disappointing support, weak outcomes, or changing priori=
ties. These require different responses.</p><p>You should not try to retain=
 someone through guilt or hiding cancellation. A member who finishes, leave=
s satisfied, and recommends you can be more valuable than a disengaged subs=
criber.</p><h3>The complete business loop</h3><p>A clear problem attracts r=
elevant people. A specific offer earns purchases. Effective onboarding crea=
tes a first result. Good teaching produces larger results. With permission,=
 those results become evidence that attracts the next suitable members. Ren=
ewal funds continued delivery.</p><p>Each part depends on the others. More =
traffic cannot repair a confusing curriculum. More lessons cannot repair an=
 audience that does not want the outcome. Diagnose the stage where progress=
 stops.</p></section><section id=3D"section-2"><h2>2 The numbers and terms =
to understand before scaling</h2><p>Revenue is money earned from sales befo=
re expenses. Gross recurring membership revenue counts the recurring member=
ship value before payment fees and delivery costs. Monthly recurring revenu=
e, or MRR, normalizes recurring charges into monthly amounts. A $1,990 annu=
al membership contributes about $165.83 in monthly recurring value; its $1,=
990 collection is cash received that month.</p><p>Keep one-time workshops, =
consulting, sponsorships, and affiliate income separate. They may fund the =
business, but they do not establish $200,000 in recurring Skool membership =
revenue. Sales taxes collected for authorities are not your operating reven=
ue.</p><h3>The minimum operating formulas</h3><ul><li>Ending paid members =
=3D starting paid members + new paid members + reactivations =E2=88=92 lost=
 paid members.</li><li>Gross MRR =3D sum of each active paid subscription=
=E2=80=99s monthly equivalent.</li><li>ARPU =3D gross MRR =C3=B7 active pai=
d members, using the same snapshot and definitions.</li><li>Monthly member =
churn =3D lost members from the starting paid cohort =C3=B7 starting paid m=
embers.</li><li>First renewal rate =3D eligible first-time members who rene=
w =C3=B7 members whose first renewal came due.</li><li>Cash CAC =3D acquisi=
tion spending =C3=B7 new paying customers attributed to that spending.</li>=
<li>Contribution per member =3D membership revenue =E2=88=92 payment costs =
=E2=88=92 channel commission =E2=88=92 refunds allowance =E2=88=92 variable=
 delivery costs.</li><li>Contribution payback =3D cash CAC =C3=B7 monthly c=
ontribution, as a rough approximation before allowing for churn.</li><li>Gr=
oss revenue retention =3D (starting MRR =E2=88=92 churned MRR =E2=88=92 dow=
ngrades) =C3=B7 starting MRR.</li><li>Net revenue retention adds upgrades a=
nd expansion to that numerator.</li></ul><p>Use a separate cohort measure f=
or people who join and leave in the same month, rather than concealing them=
 in a starting-cohort churn rate. Report cancellations scheduled for later =
and failed payments alongside the active paid snapshot.</p><p>A planning ap=
proximation is contribution lifetime value =3D monthly contribution =C3=B7 =
monthly churn. It assumes constant churn and contribution and can be badly =
misleading for a young community. Prefer observed 90-day contribution, then=
 6- and 12-month cohorts. Never spend on ads based only on a flattering the=
oretical lifetime value.</p><h3>A simple example</h3><p>Suppose you begin w=
ith 100 paid members, add 15, and lose 6 of the starting members. You end w=
ith 109 if there are no other changes. Starting-cohort member churn is 6%. =
At $149 for everyone, ending gross MRR is $16,241.</p><p>If you spent $600 =
to acquire the 15 new members, cash CAC is $40. If you spent 20 founder hou=
rs as well, those hours still matter. At an internal planning value of $60 =
an hour, fully loaded acquisition cost is ($600 + $1,200) =C3=B7 15 =3D $12=
0.</p><p>A business can have positive cash flow and still pay you poorly fo=
r your time. Track cash profit and founder hours together.</p><h3>What your=
 dashboard should prioritize</h3><p>For acquisition, track qualified page v=
isitors, qualified conversations, applications, paid starts, source, and ca=
sh CAC. For delivery, track onboarding completion, first useful artifact, p=
roject completion, review time, and unresolved blockers. For retention, tra=
ck renewal eligibility, renewals, cancellations, and reasons.</p><p>Do not =
equate a page view with learning or an approved member with a current payin=
g customer. Skool=E2=80=99s own analytics definitions include trials in mem=
ber counts and certain failing payments in MRR. Reconcile platform statisti=
cs against your paid subscription and payout records. <a href=3D"https://he=
lp.skool.com/article/216-analytics-definitions" target=3D"_blank" rel=3D"no=
opener noreferrer">Skool analytics definitions</a></p><p>Choose stable defi=
nitions for your own decisions. If your dashboard uses a different churn fo=
rmula from Skool=E2=80=99s displayed retention, name the difference rather =
than comparing the numbers as though they were identical.</p></section><sec=
tion id=3D"section-3"><h2>3 Your starting position and the recommended busi=
ness</h2><p>Your public portfolio describes full-stack development, TypeScr=
ipt and React, browser extensions, AI and ML, databases, and development to=
oling. It also shows YouTube Bookmarker and Loolite and links to educationa=
l articles. That gives you visible project material to teach from. It does =
not establish your current audience size, teaching conversion rate, or comm=
unity retention. <a href=3D"https://aadilmallick.com/" target=3D"_blank" re=
l=3D"noopener noreferrer">Your portfolio</a></p><h3>Your r=C3=A9sum=C3=A9 a=
nd what it changes</h3><p>Your AI-focused r=C3=A9sum=C3=A9 reports a comput=
er science and applied mathematics degree, structured-output evaluation res=
earch, AWS CDK infrastructure work at Unison, and testing and deployment wo=
rk at HHMI. It also lists an AI portfolio-generator project and technical w=
riting. This supports a focused offer about evaluating and shipping AI feat=
ures. <a href=3D"https://fg9lr93u3r.ufs.sh/f/M1jaWQTyrQtbbk5hOpn4HhdRWpwUj0=
82rPqgJcBb7YEIFmoS" target=3D"_blank" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer">AI-focuse=
d r=C3=A9sum=C3=A9</a></p><p>Your software engineering r=C3=A9sum=C3=A9 doc=
uments Loolite, YouTube Bookmarker, and freelance delivery for BetterCare M=
eals. It also reports historical readership for a neural-network article. U=
se that writing as a possible introduction path; past article views are not=
 a current consented audience or a pool of paying customers. <a href=3D"htt=
ps://fg9lr93u3r.ufs.sh/f/M1jaWQTyrQtbQPF4XSYFOeoVZIbSJXEWz1wUnu2yp0H9xlN5" =
target=3D"_blank" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer">Software engineering r=C3=A9s=
um=C3=A9</a></p><p>Your strongest long-term positioning is practical AI eng=
ineering with explicit evaluation, testing, and deployment. Browser extensi=
ons remain a good first capstone because their scope can be small and you h=
ave visible examples. Cloud delivery is a credible later track rather than =
a speculative new subject.</p><p>Present yourself as an early-career engine=
er and researcher teaching specific implemented skills. Your evidence suppo=
rts a strong project teacher; it does not require presenting yourself as a =
veteran executive or promising senior-engineer career outcomes.</p><div cla=
ss=3D"table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th scope=3D"col">Your evidence</th><th=
 scope=3D"col">Proposed application to the community</th></tr></thead><tbod=
y><tr><td>Evaluation and structured-output work</td><td>Teach learners to t=
est AI behavior and explain limitations</td></tr><tr><td>Full-stack project=
s and browser tools</td><td>Use functioning small products as teaching refe=
rences</td></tr><tr><td>Testing and infrastructure experience</td><td>Add r=
eliability and release readiness to project rubrics</td></tr><tr><td>Long-f=
orm technical explanations</td><td>Turn selected concepts into public teach=
ing samples</td></tr><tr><td>A real freelance engagement</td><td>Teach a pe=
rmissioned delivery case study with honest scope</td></tr></tbody></table><=
/div><p>The project=E2=80=99s attached r=C3=A9sum=C3=A9 file was not direct=
ly accessible from this chat. This tailoring uses the two r=C3=A9sum=C3=A9 =
PDFs linked from your own public r=C3=A9sum=C3=A9 page. Claims in those r=
=C3=A9sum=C3=A9s are your reported experience, not independent employer ver=
ification.</p><p>Your earlier career chats also express a strong interest i=
n teaching, extension services, cloud consulting, and technical writing. Us=
e the BetterCare Meals relationship as a possible case study with permissio=
n. Do not convert it into claims about revenue growth, client satisfaction,=
 or student earnings without the underlying evidence.</p><p>Your likely adv=
antage is teaching how a useful tool works, why its design choices matter, =
and how to finish it. Your first customers should already understand basic =
JavaScript. This reduces the amount of beginner support and makes a finishe=
d extension feasible within a short sprint.</p><p>Your initial offer should=
 be specific:</p><blockquote><p>Practical AI Software Lab helps JavaScript =
developers build, test, and explain useful browser tools and AI application=
s through guided projects and structured feedback. The first six-week sprin=
t is a small browser extension with a working demo, tests, and release docu=
mentation.</p></blockquote><p>The name is a working name. Check availabilit=
y before publishing. The initial extension can be entirely deterministic. A=
n optional AI enhancement comes after learners can ship the basic workflow.=
 This sequencing makes the technical outcome smaller and more teachable.</p=
><h3>Assumptions used until you replace them</h3><p>The launch plan assumes=
 10 to 12 hours per week, no established teaching team, no currently verifi=
ed consented audience, and up to $1,000 to $1,500 of initial business cash =
over 90 days. Your r=C3=A9sum=C3=A9 does not establish your current availab=
le hours, launch budget, email audience, or active paying-client count. The=
