n</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Avoid unsupported claims</h3><p>Do not=
 promise that learners will earn a particular income, get clients, get hire=
d, or reach a skill level you have not defined and assessed. Your own $200,=
000 target belongs in internal planning, not in a student earnings pitch.</=
p><p>The FTC=E2=80=99s action involving Lurn illustrates the consequences o=
f unfounded money-making claims in business coaching. Market the skills, su=
pport, and outcomes you can substantiate. <a href=3D"https://www.ftc.gov/ne=
ws-events/news/press-releases/2023/09/ftc-acts-stop-online-business-coachin=
g-scheme-lurn-deceiving-consumers-about-money-making-potential" target=3D"_=
blank" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer">FTC business-coaching action</a></p><h3>=
Protect your material and other people=E2=80=99s information</h3><p>Use tea=
ching code and assets you own or are licensed to distribute. Keep employer =
work, confidential client information, and private repositories out of less=
ons unless you have explicit rights and permission.</p><p>Use synthetic dat=
a for demonstrations. Keep secrets out of distributed source and browser bu=
ndles. Explain data handling before asking learners to submit work. Obtain =
separate permission for public case studies.</p><p>Do not imply an affiliat=
ion with your employer, a tool vendor, or Skool that you do not have. A tec=
hnical background supports your expertise; it does not grant permission to =
reuse someone else=E2=80=99s materials.</p><h3>Make charges understandable<=
/h3><p>Display the price, recurring frequency, expected program duration, s=
upport scope, cancellation route, and refund terms before purchase. An annu=
al plan must visibly say annual. A six-week program billed monthly should e=
xplain the expected second charge.</p><p>Before launch, review current cons=
umer requirements relevant to your buyers and your business. The operationa=
l aim is simple: buyers should know what they are agreeing to and be able t=
o leave through the promised process.</p><h3>Limit platform dependence</h3>=
<p>Back up original lessons, repositories, assessment rubrics, financial re=
cords, and consented contacts. Export only information you are entitled to =
retain and use.</p><p>Keep an orderly plan for notifying members if an outa=
ge or platform change affects delivery. Do not assume exported contact info=
rmation creates consent for unrelated marketing.</p><h3>Precommit to stoppi=
ng rules</h3><p>Pause new enrollment if a critical security issue affects t=
he teaching repository, the promised support window repeatedly fails, or ac=
tual delivery capacity is exhausted.</p><p>Pause a paid channel at its loss=
 cap or when observed contribution cannot justify its acquisition cost. Rew=
ork an offer after two clearly measured tests fail to produce enough paid i=
nterest.</p><p>If the community fails to justify its workload over two quar=
ters, choose between a finite cohort, lower-support library, consulting, or=
 closing it responsibly. Continuing because of sunk preparation time is not=
 a strategy.</p></section><section id=3D"section-23"><h2>23 Reusable messag=
